From 16f3fc0b09847e5927f92e7ef943a42979bf62bb Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Felix <52862870+devilAPI@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 19 Nov 2026 21:22:47 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 56/57] power: supply: add the SMB1351 parallel charger

The ZTE Axon 7 (and other msm8996 phones) charge with a Summit/Qualcomm
SMB1351 next to the PMI8994/PMI8996 SMBCHG: downstream gave it half of the
3 A charge current as a "usb-parallel" slave of qpnp-smbcharger. Mainline
had no driver, so charging stopped at the SMBCHG's 2 A.

This driver keeps the SMB1351's USB input suspended (I2C suspend control)
and enables it only while the primary charger (power-supplies) is online
on a DCP, in fast charge, with good health and an AICL result of at least
qcom,min-input-current-microamp, and the battery (qcom,fuel-gauge) is at
15-40 degC and below 4.15 V (off again at 4.25 V): the SMB1351 never
enters constant voltage, the primary alone tapers and terminates. The
primary's AICL result is split: up to 40 % for the SMB1351, the primary's
input limit is lowered by the same amount, so the adapter sees no more
than before. Float voltage (max 4.4 V) and charge current (1-1.5 A) are
capped below the downstream values (float + 50 mV, 50 % of 3 A). The
SMB1351's own AICL is on. Register layout from the downstream
smb1351-charger.c (The Linux Foundation).

The enable path has not been tested on a wall charger yet, so it is off by
default (module parameter enable, also smb1351_charger.enable=1 on the
command line or at runtime in /sys/module/smb1351_charger/parameters):
off, the driver only holds the SMB1351's input suspended and changes no
other register; float voltage, charge current and AICL are set on the
first enable.

